-- Mathematics: Trigonometric Functions
('MHT-CET', 'Mathematics', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'medium', 'mcq',
 'The principal value branch of sin^-1(x) is:',
 '[{"text":"[0, pi]","isCorrect":false},{"text":"[-pi/2, pi/2]","isCorrect":true},{"text":"(-pi, pi)","isCorrect":false},{"text":"[0, 2*pi]","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb,
 null, 'By convention, the principal value branch of sin^-1(x) is restricted to [-pi/2, pi/2] to make the inverse function well-defined.'),
('MHT-CET', 'Mathematics', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'easy', 'mcq',
 'The value of cos(60 degrees) is:',
 '[{"text":"1","isCorrect":false},{"text":"1/2","isCorrect":true},{"text":"sqrt(3)/2","isCorrect":false},{"text":"0","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb,
 null, 'cos(60 degrees) is a standard trigonometric value equal to 1/2.'),
('MHT-CET', 'Mathematics', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'medium', 'mcq',
 'The period of the function tan(x) is:',
 '[{"text":"pi","isCorrect":true},{"text":"2*pi","isCorrect":false},{"text":"pi/2","isCorrect":false},{"text":"4*pi","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb,
 null, 'Unlike sin(x) and cos(x), which have period 2*pi, tan(x) repeats every pi radians.'),
('MHT-CET', 'Mathematics', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'easy', 'mcq',
 'The expansion of sin(A+B) is:',
 '[{"text":"sinA*cosB + cosA*sinB","isCorrect":true},{"text":"sinA*sinB + cosA*cosB","isCorrect":false},{"text":"sinA*cosB - cosA*sinB","isCorrect":false},{"text":"cosA*cosB - sinA*sinB","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb,
 null, 'The angle addition formula for sine states sin(A+B) = sinA*cosB + cosA*sinB.'),

-- Mathematics: Vectors and 3D Geometry
('MHT-CET', 'Mathematics', 'Vectors and 3D Geometry', 'medium', 'mcq',
 'Two non-zero vectors are collinear if their cross product is:',
 '[{"text":"Equal to their dot product","isCorrect":false},{"text":"The zero vector","isCorrect":true},{"text":"Always positive","isCorrect":false},{"text":"Equal to 1","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb,
 null, 'Two non-zero vectors are collinear (parallel) precisely when the angle between them is 0 or 180 degrees, making their cross product the zero vector.'),
('MHT-CET', 'Mathematics', 'Vectors and 3D Geometry', 'easy', 'mcq',
 'The direction ratios of the line joining points (x1,y1,z1) and (x2,y2,z2) are:',
 '[{"text":"(x2-x1, y2-y1, z2-z1)","isCorrect":true},{"text":"(x1+x2, y1+y2, z1+z2)","isCorrect":false},{"text":"(x1*x2, y1*y2, z1*z2)","isCorrect":false},{"text":"(x1/x2, y1/y2, z1/z2)","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb,
 null, 'The direction ratios of a line joining two points are simply the differences of their respective coordinates: (x2-x1, y2-y1, z2-z1).'),
('MHT-CET', 'Mathematics', 'Vectors and 3D Geometry', 'medium', 'mcq',
 'The scalar triple product [a b c] of three vectors represents:',
 '[{"text":"The area of the triangle formed by the vectors","isCorrect":false},{"text":"The volume of the parallelepiped formed by the vectors","isCorrect":true},{"text":"The sum of the magnitudes of the vectors","isCorrect":false},{"text":"The angle between the vectors","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb,
 null, 'The scalar triple product a.(b x c) gives the (signed) volume of the parallelepiped formed by the three vectors as coterminous edges.'),
('MHT-CET', 'Mathematics', 'Vectors and 3D Geometry', 'easy', 'mcq',
 'If two lines in space are parallel, then their direction ratios are:',
 '[{"text":"Equal to zero","isCorrect":false},{"text":"Proportional","isCorrect":true},{"text":"Perpendicular","isCorrect":false},{"text":"Unrelated","isCorrect":false}]'::jsonb,
 null, 'Two lines are parallel exactly when their direction ratios are proportional to each other (i.e. one is a scalar multiple of the other).');
